Class: A blueprint for creating objects, defining their properties and methods.
Object: An instance of a class.


Encapsulation: Bundles data and methods within a class, hiding internal details from outside access.
Abstraction: Hides complex implementation details and exposes only necessary functionality.
Inheritance: Allows a new class to inherit properties and methods from an existing class.
Polymorphism: Enables a single interface to represent different behaviors.

Method overloading ->  Method overloading allows multiple methods with the same name but different parameters.
Method overriding ->allows a subclass to provide a specific implementation of a method that is already defined in its superclass.


from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

# Abstract class
class Bowler(ABC):

    @abstractmethod
    def bowl(self):      # only the "what", no details
        pass

# Fast bowler decides HOW to bowl
class FastBowler(Bowler):
    def bowl(self):
        print("Running fast and bowling at 145 km/h")

# Spin bowler decides HOW to bowl
class SpinBowler(Bowler):
    def bowl(self):
        print("Spinning the ball with fingers")

# Using the classes
b1 = FastBowler()
b2 = SpinBowler()

b1.bowl()   # Running fast and bowling at 145 km/h
b2.bowl()   # Spinning the ball with fingers


